course: dictionary
lesson: 2962
title: Many meanings of drop
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: One small word can have many meanings, and «drop» is a good example. Look at the words around it to find which meaning it has.
explain: To drop something is to let it fall from your hand, so you no longer have it. «Careful, you might drop the eggs.» The clue is the word fall, and the eggs are in danger of landing on the floor.
explain: Drop can also mean to go down. «The temperature dropped overnight.» Nothing fell from a hand here, so the word must mean went down. Try your meaning in the sentence: the temperature went down overnight, and that still makes sense.
fact: Drop can be a verb (drop, drops, dropped, dropping) or a noun (a drop, drops).
fact: «Drop» means to let something fall from your hand, so you no longer have it.
fact: «Drop» also means to fall or go down, as in «the temperature dropped».
fact: «A drop» can be a very small amount of liquid, like «a drop of rain», or a fall in an amount, like «a drop in price».
fact: In nature, seeds travel by many methods, and berries are eaten by birds and dropped far away from the parent plant.
exercise: «Careful, you might drop the eggs.» What does drop mean here? | Go down slowly, like the sun at night | Take someone somewhere in a car | *Let something fall from your hand | A tiny amount of liquid
exercise: «The temperature dropped overnight.» What does dropped mean? | Was measured with a ruler | Went up very fast | *Went down | Was made of glass
exercise: «She felt a drop of rain on her nose.» What is a drop here? | *A very small amount of liquid | A fall in price | A loud noise in the sky | To let something fall
test: «The price had a big drop this week.» What does drop mean here? | To leave someone in a car | A tiny amount of liquid | *A fall in an amount | To let something fall from your hand
test: «Dad will drop me off at school.» What does drop off mean? | To lose something on the way | *To take someone somewhere in a car and leave them there | To fall asleep at school | To go past the school without stopping
exam: «Saying someone cheated without proof is unfair; if nobody saw it, drop it.» What does drop it mean here? | Throw it far away | *Stop talking about it and let it go | Make it fall on purpose | Pick it up and keep it
